      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In HTML, every element has a display property. This property controls how it appears on the webpage."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<p>This is a paragraph.</p>\n2\n3<span>This is a span.</span>"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "css",
      "content": "1p, span {\n2    background: black;\n3    color: white;\n4}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Browser Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Block and Inline Elements",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In the above code, we've applied the same style to both the <p> and <span> elements."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "However, the background color of the paragraph covers the entire screen width, while the background color of the span covers only the text width."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "This difference occurs because a paragraph is a block-level element, which takes up the full width by default, while a span is an inline element that only spans the width of its content."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Let's dive deeper into how block and inline elements behave differently."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
